# quicken: a rhythm played faster and faster until it is a chord. Three
# beats against two, a ratio you hear as a rhythm; quickened a hundred
# times over, the same ratio is a fifth. Nothing changes but the speed.
#
# Two voices, one in each ear and on each axis of the scope: the left
# goes round three times while the right goes round twice. Each ticks
# as it comes round, the left with the beam at the right edge, the
# right with it at the top. Slow, you hear a hemiola, three against
# two, the two ticks landing together once a bar with the beam in the
# corner, and you see a dot wander the path between. The whole thing
# quickens, doubling every few seconds: the ticks blur into a buzz,
# the buzz has a pitch, and the voices' turning, too fast now to
# follow, is heard as two notes a fifth apart, while the dot's path
# closes into the fifth's Lissajous figure. Then it slows again and the
# chord comes apart into a rhythm. That one ratio is a rhythm, an
# interval and a shape is Stockhausen's point in "...wie die Zeit
# vergeht..." (Die Reihe 3, 1957), and what Kontakte (1960) does when
# a pitch falls until it is a beat.
#
# The buttons choose the ratio: Y two against one (the octave), A three
# against two (the fifth), B four against three (the fourth), X five
# against four (the third). Change it at any speed and hear it as both.
#
#   make tools/pad tools/scope
#   tools/pad --notes &                                 # the gamepad, every key a note
#   ./aither patches/quicken.aither | tools/scope | tools/play hw:rockchipes8388c 20

# The bar: from half a bar a second (a slow hemiola) up to 55 (a fifth on
# A2 and E3), and back. Over 90 seconds: 40 quickening, 10 held as a
# chord, 30 slowing, 10 held as a rhythm.
let loop = t mod 90
let up = clamp(loop / 40, 0, 1)
let down = clamp((loop - 50) / 30, 0, 1)
def ease(u): u * u * (3 - 2 * u)
let octaves = 6.78 * (ease(up) - ease(down))    # log2(55 / 0.5)
let bar = 0.5 * exp(octaves * 0.6931)            # bars a second

# The ratio, from the buttons; three against two to start.
$a = 3
$b = 2
$a = if midi_trig(39) > 0 then 2 else if midi_trig(36) > 0 then 3 else if midi_trig(37) > 0 then 4 else if midi_trig(38) > 0 then 5 else $a
$b = if midi_trig(39) > 0 then 1 else if midi_trig(36) > 0 then 2 else if midi_trig(37) > 0 then 3 else if midi_trig(38) > 0 then 4 else $b

# Each voice keeps its own count of turns, so a change of speed or of
# ratio never jumps. Once it is a chord the right runs a tenth of a
# turn a second ahead, so the closed figure turns slowly; while it is a
# rhythm the two stay locked.
$pa = 0.0
$pb = 0.0
$pa = $pa + $a * bar * dt
$pb = $pb + ($b * bar + 0.1 * clamp((bar - 20) / 20, 0, 1)) * dt

# A tick each time a voice comes round: a burst of noise 1.5 ms long.
# Past 20 bars a second they have fused into a buzz, and they fade
# out by 40, leaving the voices' own tones.
def tick(turns, rate):
  let since = (turns mod 1) / rate
  noise() * exp(0 - since * 700)
let ticks = clamp(2 - bar / 20, 0, 1)
let left = cos(TAU * $pa) * 0.7 + tick($pa, $a * bar) * 0.35 * ticks
let right = cos(TAU * $pb) * 0.7 + tick($pb, $b * bar) * 0.35 * ticks

[left, right] * 0.75 |> tanh
